narratr License
Version 1.0

Copyright (c) 2026 Aman Khandelwal (https://github.com/amankhandelwal/narratr)

narratr turns a document into a narrated video. It is two things together: a
program that narrates, renders and assembles the video, and a Claude skill
(the `skill/` directory) that writes the script the program renders. Both are
the original work of the Licensor, released so that people can use them
freely. This license keeps it that way: free to use, free to share, and always
credited.

This is a source-available license. It is modelled on the permissive shape of
the MIT and Apache 2.0 licenses, but it adds conditions those licenses do not
have (Sections 3 to 6). It is not an OSI-approved open source license.


1. Definitions

"Licensor" means Aman Khandelwal, the copyright holder.

"Software" means everything in this repository -- source code, the Claude
skill, prompts, schemas, templates, documentation, configuration and assets --
except the Third-Party Components listed in Section 8.

"Derivative Work" means any work that is based on, or incorporates, all or any
part of the Software, whether modified or not. A fork, a port to another
language, a copy of the skill or its prompts, and a program that reuses the
Software's code are all Derivative Works.

"Output" means any video, audio, image, caption file or other media produced,
in whole or in part, by the Software or by a Derivative Work.

"Intro" means the title card that opens every Output: the narratr mark
(`render/remotion/public/narratr-mark.png` and `assets/Narratr.png`), the
narratr name, the intro music (`assets/intro.mp3`), and their layout, timing
and animation as implemented by the Software (`render/remotion/src/Intro.tsx`
and `narratr/intro.py`). The document title shown on the card is supplied by
the user and is not part of the Intro.

"Commercial Distribution" means selling, renting, licensing or sublicensing
the Software or a Derivative Work, or charging any fee, subscription, credit
or other consideration for access to it or its functionality, including
offering it as a hosted or managed service.


